1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
You can call them gizmos.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
The Popeil pocket fisherman that makes a great thing.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:14,000
You can call them doohickeys.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Perfectly simple operation.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Just don't call them useless.

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, gadgets on Modern Marvel.

7
00:00:46,406 --> 00:00:53,040
Gadget. Just the sound of the word makes you want to pick it up and try it out.

8
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,520
Hey, good looking. We'll be back to pick you up later.

9
00:00:57,521 --> 00:01:05,520
Gadgets have spawned entire industries and even helped make the infomercial a television staple.

10
00:01:05,521 --> 00:01:07,520
Do I make product that's good?

11
00:01:07,521 --> 00:01:08,520
Wow.

12
00:01:08,521 --> 00:01:11,520
Now I don't suggest you do that at home.

13
00:01:11,521 --> 00:01:22,520
Webster's defines gadget as an often small mechanical or electronic device with a practical use, but often thought of as a novelty.

14
00:01:22,521 --> 00:01:29,360
The gadgeteers who invent such devices like the self-propelled inner tube dream of unique

15
00:01:29,361 --> 00:01:34,360
ways to make life, if not easier, a little more interesting.

16
00:01:34,361 --> 00:01:39,300
They also take the extra step of bringing their vision to fruition.

17
00:01:39,301 --> 00:01:46,280
Once a year, 18 especially talented gadgeteers are called to one of the world's oldest retailers

18
00:01:46,281 --> 00:01:50,280
of gadgets, the Hamaker Schlemmer store in Manhattan.

19
00:01:50,281 --> 00:01:53,044
Amitra Schlimmer is 151 years old.

20
00:01:53,045 --> 00:01:58,980
We opened our first store down in the Bowery section of New York in 1848.

21
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:04,660
That was, for those of you who don't remember, that was when Zachary Taylor was elected president.

22
00:02:05,420 --> 00:02:11,080
It's here that the final judging of the year-long Search for Invention contest occurs.

23
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:16,900
The 18 finalists were selected for more than 350 entries.

24
00:02:16,901 --> 00:02:23,300
To be eligible, contestants must have a patent and a working prototype of their invention.

25
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:32,800
It's our way of saying thank you to all of the inventors who work against very difficult odds to bring their ideas to the marketplace.

26
00:02:33,640 --> 00:02:39,840
The finalists from all across the nation are not necessarily engineers or MIT graduates.

27
00:02:40,620 --> 00:02:43,400
Donald Martell is a school bus driver.

28
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:51,140
His invention, the airtight container, was born from a desire to preserve a baseball autographed by Mickey Mantle.

29
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:54,460
Everybody thought I was nuts, and I just started drawing it.

30
00:02:54,940 --> 00:02:58,840
Somebody said, I know an engineer could do a better thing on your drawings, and they sent me there.

31
00:02:58,900 --> 00:03:00,413
And the engineer said, I love this.

32
00:03:00,414 --> 00:03:02,660
I got a guy who does models, and they sent me there.

33
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:07,320
The model maker says, you know, I got a guy who might even be able to produce it, and they sent me there.

34
00:03:07,460 --> 00:03:10,460
And it's taken six years, but nothing's ever gone wrong.

35
00:03:10,461 --> 00:03:18,280
Martel's gadget utilizes a pump to vacuum seal containers designed to preserve collectibles

36
00:03:18,281 --> 00:03:22,900
ranging from hockey pucks and baseballs to jewelry stamps and coins.

37
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:25,580
Now it's basically vacuum sealed.

38
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:28,140
You can't separate it from the container from the base.

39
00:03:28,480 --> 00:03:29,980
So what we do is we just have a little button,

40
00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:33,740
you're taking the base off, and now you have a vacuum sealed item.

41
00:03:34,340 --> 00:03:39,120
Thomas Kehoe invented a gadget to solve a dilemma in his life as well.

42
00:03:40,120 --> 00:03:42,540
He is a lifelong stutterer.

43
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:47,800
I went through seven stuttering therapy programs over 15 years,

44
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:52,300
and really nothing helped me improve my speech.

45
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:55,380
So I had to find a way to talk fluently.

46
00:03:56,160 --> 00:03:57,540
Several years earlier.

47
00:03:57,960 --> 00:04:05,540
My fastest speech was ten times slower than a non-stutterer.

48
00:04:05,541 --> 00:04:11,740
Kehoe invented an earphone device allowing stutterers to hear themselves on a slight delay.

49
00:04:12,740 --> 00:04:16,780
When attempting to keep pace with his voice, he stopped stuttering.

50
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:19,840
I'm normal sounding speech and you're talking as fast as you want.

51
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:22,180
Am I talking pretty fast now? I'm talking pretty fast.

52
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:29,380
Now he has invented a similar gadget that allows a stutterer or a shy person to speak in public.

53
00:04:30,440 --> 00:04:35,260
Along with the delay, the playback actually alters the tone of the voice.

54
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:39,860
prompting the speaker to exude confidence or enthusiasm.

55
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:44,620
I'm going to program it for confident authority,

56
00:04:45,020 --> 00:04:48,400
which makes you slow down, sound more confident.

57
00:04:48,820 --> 00:04:53,920
The other mental state is happy enthusiasm.

58
00:04:54,380 --> 00:04:58,260
You put this on and you talk a little faster and you sound more enthusiastic

59
00:04:58,261 --> 00:05:01,840
and when you tell jokes, your jokes sound really funny to you at least.

60
00:05:01,841 --> 00:05:09,500
Within this hour, we'll meet many of the other finalists and find out who will walk away with the grand prize.

61
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:19,060
While some of these contestants may have spent years developing their inventions, the history of gadgets spans thousands of years.

62
00:05:20,100 --> 00:05:22,820
The word itself, though, is considerably younger.

63
00:05:22,821 --> 00:05:27,580
It didn't enter the English lexicon until the presentation of a gift

64
00:05:27,581 --> 00:05:32,880
that, while decidedly not a gadget, did require some assembly.

65
00:05:34,580 --> 00:05:40,300
Packed in more than 200 crates, the Statue of Liberty arrived in New York in 1885.

66
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:46,820
This gift from France was fabricated by the Parisian firm of Gaget Gautier AC.

67
00:05:46,821 --> 00:05:53,380
It was Monsieur Gaget who came up with the idea of selling small replicas of Lady Liberty

68
00:05:53,381 --> 00:05:59,000
to excited New Yorkers who called the souvenirs not Gagets, but gadgets.

69
00:06:01,140 --> 00:06:06,500
While only a 19th century word, husband and wife authors Michael and Mary Woods

70
00:06:06,501 --> 00:06:10,120
have written a series of books on the origins of technology

71
00:06:10,121 --> 00:06:14,340
and found that gadgets permeated ancient civilizations.

72
00:06:14,341 --> 00:06:21,340
The Chinese magic spoon, a fascinating gadget that dates to 2600 BC.

73
00:06:21,341 --> 00:06:26,340
If you take an ordinary spoon and throw it on the ground onto a hard surface,

74
00:06:26,341 --> 00:06:29,340
the spoon is going to bounce and shake and finally come to rest.

75
00:06:29,341 --> 00:06:33,340
The magic spoon acted as if it was haunted.

76
00:06:33,341 --> 00:06:38,340
When a magic spoon hit the ground, the bowl always aligned itself in one direction,

77
00:06:38,341 --> 00:06:43,340
while the handle pointed in another, and it was always north.

78
00:06:43,341 --> 00:06:49,340
Magic spoons were made out of magnetite, a natural magnetic material.

79
00:06:49,341 --> 00:06:53,340
People used it as a gadget, a toy and an amusement for hundreds of years.

80
00:06:53,341 --> 00:06:59,340
And then someone realized, gee whiz, the bowl points one direction,

81
00:06:59,341 --> 00:07:01,340
the handle always points in the other direction.

82
00:07:01,341 --> 00:07:04,340
Maybe this can help me find the way.

83
00:07:04,341 --> 00:07:11,340
By the 13th century, the Chinese would share the magic spoon with the rest of the world, as the compass.

84
00:07:11,341 --> 00:07:20,340
Perhaps the ancient world's greatest gadgeteer was a Greek physicist who lived in the first century AD, Hero of Alexandria.

85
00:07:20,341 --> 00:07:24,086
Hero really was the Rube Goldberg of antiquity.

86
00:07:24,087 --> 00:07:31,340
He left us drawings of about a hundred different gadgets and gizmos and mechanical devices.

87
00:07:31,341 --> 00:07:37,340
Many of Hero's inventions were used not only during his lifetime, but ours.

88
00:07:37,341 --> 00:07:43,500
One example was called the sacrificial vessel, which flows only when money is introduced.

89
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:51,120
Today it's no longer thought of as a gadget, but the vending machine drink dispenser.

90
00:07:53,080 --> 00:07:56,980
Hero's greatest gadget may have been little more than a source of amusement.

91
00:07:57,760 --> 00:08:05,220
He called it the air ball, a hollow metal ball pierced by two hollow tubes was suspended over a small cauldron.

92
00:08:05,221 --> 00:08:13,020
When heated, the ball would rapidly spin, delighting his friends, in the days before television.

93
00:08:13,560 --> 00:08:22,540
A modern engineer recreated the air ball a few years ago, and he clocked it and found that it spawned at 1,500 revolutions per minute.

94
00:08:23,160 --> 00:08:26,780
It was the fastest rotating object in the ancient world.

95
00:08:26,781 --> 00:08:33,780
That airball was the world's first steam engine, which in the 1800s fostered the Industrial Revolution,

96
00:08:33,781 --> 00:08:36,780
was used in locomotives and steamboats.

97
00:08:36,781 --> 00:08:42,780
While the physics behind Hero's airball would later revolutionize technology,

98
00:08:42,781 --> 00:08:47,780
the 1800 years in between would see the introduction of countless gadgets.

99
00:08:47,781 --> 00:08:53,780
Many of those, like these fancy 19th century sheep shears,

100
00:08:53,781 --> 00:08:57,820
can be found inside this seven-story castle.

101
00:08:58,700 --> 00:09:01,620
The Mercer Museum in Doylestown, Pennsylvania

102
00:09:01,621 --> 00:09:05,280
is home to thousands of gadgets dating from the Middle Ages

103
00:09:05,281 --> 00:09:07,240
through the early 20th century.

104
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:11,440
It is a virtual shrine to all those gadgeteers

105
00:09:11,441 --> 00:09:13,980
who sought to build a better mousetrap.

106
00:09:14,400 --> 00:09:17,220
And in the mid-1800s, someone did.

107
00:09:18,620 --> 00:09:20,840
Once the mouse was inside the cage,

108
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:22,840
the mouse could be kept as a family pet

109
00:09:22,841 --> 00:09:28,240
and exercise on a regular basis by simply passing through the hole, the doorway here,

110
00:09:28,520 --> 00:09:32,120
into the exercise wheel where the mouse could run his little legs off.

111
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:37,820
A far less humane gadget was the terrapin scoop.

112
00:09:38,900 --> 00:09:42,280
This is an example of a gadget that got to be too much of a good thing.

113
00:09:42,740 --> 00:09:48,040
In the middle of the 19th century, freshwater terrapin, or turtles, were quite a delicacy.

114
00:09:48,460 --> 00:09:52,940
This was a device that was made by Andrew Rickert of Doylestown for hunting terrapin.

115
00:09:53,040 --> 00:09:54,740
He reworked one of his farm tools.

116
00:09:55,340 --> 00:10:00,720
The end of this is made out of manure fork with the tines brought together at the top to form a scoop.

117
00:10:01,260 --> 00:10:08,860
When terrapin hunting, Rickert would poke about in the mud in the bottom of a river with the pointed end to locate the terrapin

118
00:10:08,861 --> 00:10:10,780
and then scoop it out with the other end.

119
00:10:10,781 --> 00:10:17,800
like the terrapin scoop the demand for this gadget has diminished over the past few hundred years as

120
00:10:17,801 --> 00:10:24,860
well today we don't need to make our own pegs but in the days before walmart when you made

121
00:10:24,861 --> 00:10:30,660
everything from your own rakes to clothing hooks you couldn't have enough pegs the moving part is

122
00:10:30,661 --> 00:10:37,900
the human the human arm with this the peg maker consists of a sharpened cylinder embedded in a

123
00:10:37,901 --> 00:10:38,927
piece of wood.

124
00:10:38,928 --> 00:10:44,940
As I put a new billet of wood up here to the top of the peg maker and pound it in,

125
00:10:44,941 --> 00:10:49,382
what that will do is push out the previous peg that I've made.

126
00:10:49,383 --> 00:10:51,460
Many of the gadgets that have

127
00:10:51,461 --> 00:10:57,440
survived to modern times are often found in the kitchen, and some, like the apple peeler,

128
00:10:57,560 --> 00:11:00,426
were made in a huge variety of styles.

129
00:11:00,427 --> 00:11:04,800
The Mercer has more than 60 different apple peelers in its

130
00:11:04,801 --> 00:11:09,040
collection, including one used for demonstrations.

131
00:11:09,041 --> 00:11:13,260
You place the apple on the prongs here.

132
00:11:13,261 --> 00:11:16,080
Set the blade in so that it's starting to cut the skin.

133
00:11:20,120 --> 00:11:24,860
And then guide the blade manually as you're turning the apple.

134
00:11:24,861 --> 00:11:27,840
The apple peeler, like thousands of other objects

135
00:11:27,841 --> 00:11:30,740
in the Mercer collection, were fashioned by hand

136
00:11:30,741 --> 00:11:36,160
from an enterprising gadgeteer with the need to meet.

137
00:11:36,161 --> 00:11:41,940
These gadgets were sometimes one-of-a-kind objects used solely by the maker.

138
00:11:41,941 --> 00:11:44,880
But five years prior to the Civil War,

139
00:11:44,881 --> 00:11:50,460
a pair of German immigrants would lay the foundation for how unique gadgets of the future

140
00:11:50,461 --> 00:11:57,060
would be marketed to a massive gadget-craving public.

141
00:11:57,061 --> 00:12:01,640
In 1858, Walter Hunt created a gadget in under three hours,

142
00:12:02,060 --> 00:12:06,080
then sold the patent for about $300 to repay debts.

143
00:12:06,700 --> 00:12:11,000
Since then, the modern safety pin has made countless millions.

144
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:14,680
Gadgets will return on Modern Marvels.

145
00:12:15,460 --> 00:12:19,340
We now return to Gadgets on Modern Marvels.

146
00:12:21,500 --> 00:12:25,640
Back at the Hamaker-Schlemmer Search for Invention contest,

147
00:12:25,641 --> 00:12:32,360
Gadgeteers compete for not only a $5,000 grand prize, but for an even bigger dream.

148
00:12:32,780 --> 00:12:40,660
The possibility that this venerable retailer of gadgets will include their invention here on the shelves of their Manhattan store.

149
00:12:40,661 --> 00:12:51,725
It was in this building that Hamaker Slimmer introduced the first pop-up toaster, the first electric shaver, the steam iron, and the first cordless electric

150
00:12:51,726 --> 00:12:55,200
toothbrush, just to name a few of the inventions.

151
00:12:55,640 --> 00:13:04,457
Hoping to add his name and his invention to this pedigree is John Picone, a quality assurance representative for the U.S.

152
00:13:04,458 --> 00:13:06,060
Department of Defense.

153
00:13:07,260 --> 00:13:12,380
Picone took a common everyday hand tool, the wrench, and turned it into a gadget.

154
00:13:13,480 --> 00:13:15,561
This is the powered adjustable wrench.

155
00:13:15,562 --> 00:13:19,120
You have a switch here which actuates the door to open and close.

156
00:13:19,121 --> 00:13:26,300
Picone had grown tired of trying to manually adjust wrenches and tight cramped quarters

157
00:13:26,301 --> 00:13:29,340
when the idea for the power wrench came to him.

158
00:13:29,980 --> 00:13:34,780
It's powered by two AA batteries and Picone envisions a day when astronauts,

159
00:13:35,240 --> 00:13:39,220
hampered by their bulky gloves, will use his wrench in space.

160
00:13:40,440 --> 00:13:43,400
Some gadgets just have a way of begging the question,

161
00:13:43,401 --> 00:13:45,460
why didn't anybody think of this sooner?

162
00:13:45,461 --> 00:13:52,460
There's no guarantee that any of the contest entrants will have their invention marketed by Homaker.

163
00:13:52,461 --> 00:14:00,460
For the decision to carry a particular gadget such as the Sibot,

164
00:14:00,461 --> 00:14:05,460
a computerized robot that can deliver coffee or sing the national anthem,

165
00:14:05,461 --> 00:14:11,460
is up to company president Bill Booth and CEO Richard Tinberg.

166
00:14:11,461 --> 00:14:13,460
Well, I still think it's a great watch.

167
00:14:13,461 --> 00:14:14,260
a great watch.

168
00:14:14,580 --> 00:14:17,180
Amacher Schlemmer is a company that is dedicated

169
00:14:17,181 --> 00:14:20,940
to finding the products that are the best in their category

170
00:14:20,941 --> 00:14:22,840
and those that are truly unique.

171
00:14:23,240 --> 00:14:26,440
And we specialize primarily in functional products

172
00:14:26,441 --> 00:14:28,420
and some would say functional gadgetry.

173
00:14:29,280 --> 00:14:31,960
Amacher Schlemmer evolved into gadgetry

174
00:14:31,961 --> 00:14:35,380
from this hardware store opened in the Bowery section

175
00:14:35,381 --> 00:14:37,600
of New York in 1848.

176
00:14:38,380 --> 00:14:42,500
That was 13 years prior to the outbreak of the Civil War.

177
00:14:43,460 --> 00:14:50,460
William Schlemmer was only a 12-year-old newly arrived German immigrant when he began working here.

178
00:14:50,461 --> 00:14:53,460
His uncle, William Tolner, owned the store.

179
00:14:53,461 --> 00:15:00,460
By 1867, Schlemmer was part owner along with fellow German immigrant Alfred Hamacher.

180
00:15:00,461 --> 00:15:08,460
In 1881, Hamacher Schlemmer expanded their marketing efforts by introducing their first mail-order catalog.

181
00:15:08,461 --> 00:15:13,020
Today it is the longest continually published catalog in the United States.

182
00:15:13,860 --> 00:15:21,340
At the turn of the century, the catalog began to reflect the propensity for unique items that would later distinguish the store.

183
00:15:22,120 --> 00:15:27,940
It advertised the first tool set for the newest technological wonder, the horseless carriage.

184
00:15:28,900 --> 00:15:34,620
The motorist touring kit was sold when there were only 600 automobiles in all of New York City.

185
00:15:34,621 --> 00:15:42,140
In 1926, Homacher Schlemmer moved to its current location on 57th Street in Manhattan.

186
00:15:42,940 --> 00:15:47,280
The move brought a change in both clientele and product.

187
00:15:47,900 --> 00:15:51,760
Given the boom and building that was happening in the latter 20s

188
00:15:51,761 --> 00:15:55,240
and the way that people wanted to furnish their homes,

189
00:15:55,340 --> 00:16:00,820
it was only natural that we would expand and add a wider variety of products to serve that clientele.

190
00:16:00,821 --> 00:16:07,520
When Hamaker shifted their focus from hardware to new luxury gadgets, they became the first

191
00:16:07,521 --> 00:16:12,060
to introduce dozens of what are today considered common household products.

192
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:21,500
In 1937, one such product would immortalize the name of a popular bandleader.

193
00:16:22,160 --> 00:16:24,840
Fred Waring was a Hamaker customer for a long time.

194
00:16:24,841 --> 00:16:29,580
He was also the famous bandleader of Fred Waring and the Pennsylvanians.

195
00:16:29,581 --> 00:16:35,880
and his wife had had dental surgery and had difficulty eating her food.

196
00:16:36,660 --> 00:16:41,000
In response, Waring invented a device to blend his wife's food,

197
00:16:41,240 --> 00:16:43,620
making it softer and easier for her to eat.

198
00:16:44,640 --> 00:16:48,560
Hamaker began marketing the Waring Blender in 1937.

199
00:16:49,440 --> 00:16:52,940
It would be one of the top-selling blenders for decades to come.

200
00:16:54,020 --> 00:16:58,320
Over the years, other gadgets first introduced by Hamaker Schlemmer

201
00:16:58,321 --> 00:17:01,320
would serve as a history book of American pop culture.

202
00:17:02,420 --> 00:17:05,800
They range from the first bathroom scale in 1937

203
00:17:05,801 --> 00:17:09,800
to the first electric pencil sharpener in 1947

204
00:17:09,801 --> 00:17:13,680
and the portable blow dryer in 1953.

205
00:17:14,720 --> 00:17:18,480
The 1960s brought the first cordless phone.

206
00:17:19,120 --> 00:17:21,920
It was just like any other rotary phone of the time,

207
00:17:22,420 --> 00:17:24,800
except an antenna replaced the cord.

208
00:17:24,801 --> 00:17:32,200
The first pocket calculator, priced at a whopping $300, was introduced in 1972.

209
00:17:33,480 --> 00:17:39,600
A year later, the first automatic coffee maker, Mr. Coffee, made its debut.

210
00:17:41,140 --> 00:17:47,080
Today, a variety of robotic gadgets can be found at Homaker, including the RoboMow.

211
00:17:47,081 --> 00:17:53,260
This self-propelled, computerized lawnmower can be programmed to learn the parameters of a backyard,

212
00:17:53,800 --> 00:17:55,160
and even do the trimming.

213
00:17:57,400 --> 00:18:01,220
So where do all these ideas and gadgets come from?

214
00:18:01,800 --> 00:18:06,840
They're searched down and sorted out by Homaker-Schlemmer buyers like Mark Sheldon.

215
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:11,140
While searching for an entirely different product,

216
00:18:11,620 --> 00:18:16,200
Sheldon stumbled upon an Internet site for this device, the Zybernaut.

217
00:18:16,860 --> 00:18:21,480
The Zybernaut is a wearable computer, recently launched by Homaker.

218
00:18:22,080 --> 00:18:28,160
This computer is just like a desktop or a laptop, but it's completely wireless, completely wearable.

219
00:18:28,680 --> 00:18:31,220
You wear your CPU, which is under two pounds.

220
00:18:31,221 --> 00:18:34,280
You wear a headgear, which is just over a pound.

221
00:18:34,780 --> 00:18:37,100
The whole thing together is just under four pounds.

222
00:18:37,520 --> 00:18:41,200
It's faster than most desktops, at least my desktop.

223
00:18:41,201 --> 00:18:48,420
The Zybernaut's monitor is positioned about two inches from the user's eye, yet it projects

224
00:18:48,421 --> 00:18:51,840
the illusion of a 15-inch screen.

225
00:18:51,841 --> 00:18:58,420
It even comes with a portable camera, capable of transmitting its data to other computers.

226
00:18:58,421 --> 00:19:02,520
And the Zybernaut can be programmed to respond to voice commands.

227
00:19:02,521 --> 00:19:09,180
Talking to gadgets or hearing from gadgets is especially popular at Homaker.

228
00:19:09,181 --> 00:19:13,500
This pen is not really a pen, it's a digital voice recorder.

229
00:19:13,501 --> 00:19:15,740
Remember to program a talking light switch.

230
00:19:17,480 --> 00:19:20,280
Remember to program a talking light switch.

231
00:19:21,320 --> 00:19:27,600
It comes in handy for remembering to program other gadgets, like the talking light switch plate.

232
00:19:29,620 --> 00:19:32,620
Remember to bring digital recording pen to work today.

233
00:19:32,621 --> 00:19:39,760
Remember to bring digital recording pen to work today.

234
00:19:41,060 --> 00:19:47,620
While other gadgets like the laser beam golf putter that offers a direct line of sight to the hole,

235
00:19:48,620 --> 00:19:56,360
or even the beeping wallet that sets off an alarm whenever you remove a credit card, define Homaker.

236
00:19:56,361 --> 00:20:05,020
The real attention getters are those larger than life items that possess a gadget sensibility.

237
00:20:06,720 --> 00:20:09,260
This is my idea of the ultimate gadget.

238
00:20:10,040 --> 00:20:16,620
What else would you call a tricycle built for seven other than a gadget of social transportation?

239
00:20:17,840 --> 00:20:23,180
This one just sold for $16,000 at Homaker's Chicago store.

240
00:20:23,181 --> 00:20:30,380
It was purchased by this pizza chain owner from San Antonio, Texas, as a birthday gift for his wife.

241
00:20:32,920 --> 00:20:40,900
The viability of any gadget, from the septuplet tricycle to this fail-proof corkscrew,

242
00:20:41,380 --> 00:20:46,040
is subject to countless social, economic, and intellectual factors.

243
00:20:46,820 --> 00:20:49,840
Some will withstand the test of time.

244
00:20:49,841 --> 00:20:55,840
Others will go down in history as just being downright wacky.

245
00:20:57,840 --> 00:21:05,660
In 1914, Tsar Nicholas II purchased one of every item in the Homager Schlemmer catalog for that year.

246
00:21:06,440 --> 00:21:08,840
Gadgets will return on Modern Marvels.

247
00:21:10,640 --> 00:21:14,500
Gadgets often result from the inventor's hobby or career.

248
00:21:15,240 --> 00:21:17,840
For John Lawrence of Campbellsville, Pennsylvania,

249
00:21:17,841 --> 00:21:25,560
It was a combination of his vocation, dentistry, and his avocation, vintage automobile restoration,

250
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:29,580
that propelled him to the Search for Invention contest finals.

251
00:21:30,820 --> 00:21:37,780
Once the dentist cleans your teeth, this device cleans the material and the gunk away from the undercarriage of your car.

252
00:21:38,800 --> 00:21:44,960
Lawrence's portable automobile undercarriage washer turns an otherwise strenuous job

253
00:21:44,961 --> 00:21:48,140
into one no more difficult than watering the lawn.

254
00:21:49,220 --> 00:21:53,900
It works by connecting a common garden hose gun nozzle to the undercarriage rod.

255
00:21:54,540 --> 00:21:58,720
The water mixes with the cleaning solution stored in a pressurized chamber.

256
00:21:59,660 --> 00:22:04,400
A squeeze of the nozzle shoots a powerful fan-shaped spray beneath the car.

257
00:22:06,500 --> 00:22:10,480
When Craig Freeman, a firefighter from Southern California,

258
00:22:10,740 --> 00:22:13,400
and his wife Pamela conceived of their gadget,

259
00:22:13,401 --> 00:22:18,020
they were less concerned with gunk than they were with junk.

260
00:22:18,980 --> 00:22:19,760
Junk food.

261
00:22:21,100 --> 00:22:26,380
What it is exactly is a countertop refrigerator that replaces the cookie jar.

262
00:22:26,620 --> 00:22:27,920
It's for vegetables and fruits.

263
00:22:28,520 --> 00:22:32,340
It's the convenience of having a healthy snack as opposed to having a sugar snack.

264
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:34,700
Plugged into a 110 outlet,

265
00:22:35,360 --> 00:22:39,080
the veggie jar maintains refrigerator temperatures on the inside

266
00:22:39,081 --> 00:22:42,600
while remaining at room temperature on the outside.

267
00:22:43,400 --> 00:22:45,371
What the Freemans share with Dr.

268
00:22:45,372 --> 00:22:48,820
Lawrence, besides an interest in preventing tooth decay,

269
00:22:49,260 --> 00:22:52,880
is that they have both been through the United States patent process,

270
00:22:53,160 --> 00:22:56,580
which takes place in this Washington, D.C. building.

271
00:22:57,480 --> 00:23:02,360
The patents for the undercarriage washer and the countertop veggie jar

272
00:23:02,361 --> 00:23:09,380
are among the nearly 7 million patents that have been filed since the system was established in 1790

273
00:23:09,381 --> 00:23:11,720
under President George Washington.

274
00:23:13,400 --> 00:23:19,760
Navigating this maze of shelves and files is the job of inventors and patent attorneys,

275
00:23:19,761 --> 00:23:25,840
who search out prior patents to determine if their idea is truly unique or not.

276
00:23:28,260 --> 00:23:33,660
Mike Colitz is a long-time patent attorney who, in decades of conducting patent searches,

277
00:23:33,820 --> 00:23:36,800
has come across more than a few that have made him smile.

278
00:23:38,240 --> 00:23:42,320
He has compiled his favorites both in a book, Wacky Patents,

279
00:23:42,321 --> 00:23:47,040
and on his website, which offers a wacky patent of the month.

280
00:23:48,160 --> 00:23:52,060
The inventions that I research, they're not really wacky.

281
00:23:52,240 --> 00:23:54,860
It's just that through the passage of time,

282
00:23:55,700 --> 00:24:00,760
technologies which were of importance to people of days past

283
00:24:00,761 --> 00:24:03,780
may, in today's light, appear to us to be wacky.

284
00:24:04,180 --> 00:24:07,540
And then you take it a step further and say to yourself,

285
00:24:07,541 --> 00:24:15,400
if people today consider the technical experts of our grandparents to be wacky,

286
00:24:16,080 --> 00:24:23,020
what cherished ideas do we have that our grandchildren are going to consider wacky?

287
00:24:23,380 --> 00:24:26,680
We may not survive long enough to know the answer.

288
00:24:27,280 --> 00:24:31,440
However, the fear of missing the future due to premature burial

289
00:24:31,441 --> 00:24:35,080
led to this wacky patent filed in 1891,

290
00:24:35,081 --> 00:24:38,100
the enunciator for the supposed dead.

291
00:24:38,780 --> 00:24:42,740
We just assume that there was a problem a hundred years ago

292
00:24:42,741 --> 00:24:45,300
of people being buried alive,

293
00:24:45,800 --> 00:24:48,280
supposedly dead, but actually alive.

294
00:24:48,720 --> 00:24:52,280
The enunciator consisted of an above-ground bell

295
00:24:52,281 --> 00:24:54,760
attached to a cord inside a coffin.

296
00:24:55,640 --> 00:25:00,000
If the person inside awoke from what was only a misdiagnosed coma,

297
00:25:00,001 --> 00:25:04,360
they could alert cemetery visitors to their underground dilemma.

298
00:25:05,080 --> 00:25:15,080
While eyeglasses for chickens might appear wacky to some, they actually solve a real problem and were patented in 1903.

299
00:25:15,081 --> 00:25:25,080
It looks like it's a, uh, glasses and a strapped hole of glasses on to a chicken to protect the chicken's eyes or give better vision.

300
00:25:25,081 --> 00:25:37,480
But if you read the text of the patent, you will find out that apparently chickens being birds are in fact bird-brained, not the brightest creatures that God created.

301
00:25:38,100 --> 00:25:45,200
And at feeding time, the chickens go around, peck, peck, peck, picking up the particulate food to eat.

302
00:25:45,420 --> 00:25:53,540
And very, very frequently, I'm told, and we learn from the patent, a chicken would pluck out the eyes of another chicken.

303
00:25:53,541 --> 00:25:58,720
Though wacky in appearance, chicken glasses were a useful gadget.

304
00:25:59,500 --> 00:26:04,260
Variations have been worn by some intellectually challenged chickens ever since.

305
00:26:05,680 --> 00:26:13,380
However, the usefulness of the patent number 586,025 has yet to be realized.

306
00:26:14,600 --> 00:26:23,080
The combined grocer's package, cheese slicer and grater, and fly trap and mouse trap.

307
00:26:23,540 --> 00:26:28,880
This combination gadget invented in 1897 is what it claims to be.

308
00:26:29,820 --> 00:26:33,920
A cheese grater and slicer that also kills vermin.

309
00:26:34,660 --> 00:26:38,720
If the patent office had the same knowledge of food sanitation it has today,

310
00:26:39,160 --> 00:26:41,980
such a patent would probably not have been issued.

311
00:26:43,460 --> 00:26:49,540
However, the preparation and eating of food has consistently been the inspiration for unique gadgets.

312
00:26:49,541 --> 00:26:57,000
In the 1920s, newsreels frequently reported on the works of a now forgotten inventor,

313
00:26:57,840 --> 00:27:04,600
Russell Oaks, also known as Professor I.M. Nuts, from Waukesha, Wisconsin.

314
00:27:05,600 --> 00:27:09,700
His grapefruit mask prevented the eater from being squirted in the eye.

315
00:27:10,800 --> 00:27:15,620
However, his pièce de résistance was the automatic donut dunker.

316
00:27:15,621 --> 00:27:22,680
You bring the donut to your mouth and eat like that without dripping any coffee on the tablecloth.

317
00:27:24,060 --> 00:27:29,340
Unfortunately for Oakes, his passion for the donut dunker was not shared by the public.

318
00:27:30,480 --> 00:27:35,220
Like the vast majority of gadgets, it would not stand the test of time.

319
00:27:37,180 --> 00:27:40,900
This is a gadget that has stood the test.

320
00:27:40,901 --> 00:27:46,106
You can even pick your teeth and open your beer with a Swiss Army knife.

321
00:27:46,107 --> 00:27:47,480
It does everything.

322
00:27:47,760 --> 00:27:54,980
The Victorinox Corporation has been producing the multifunction, spring-loaded Swiss Army knife since 1891.

323
00:27:55,860 --> 00:28:04,680
It was invented by the company's founder, Carl Elzener, from the cutlery factory he operated out of this house in Ebach, Switzerland.

324
00:28:05,520 --> 00:28:10,100
And Elzener did, in fact, invent the knife for the Swiss Army.

325
00:28:10,900 --> 00:28:14,720
A couple years ago, I was buying a Swiss Army knife for one of my kids for Christmas,

326
00:28:14,721 --> 00:28:20,000
and suddenly when I'm at the cash register, I look at this thing, and I realize the Swiss are neutral.

327
00:28:20,460 --> 00:28:21,600
What's a Swiss Army?

328
00:28:21,940 --> 00:28:24,540
So I actually went home and called the Swiss Embassy,

329
00:28:25,120 --> 00:28:27,340
and they told me, yes, they do have an army.

330
00:28:27,760 --> 00:28:29,700
It's for defensive purposes only.

331
00:28:30,540 --> 00:28:34,040
Today, the knife is still used by not only the Swiss Army,

332
00:28:34,200 --> 00:28:36,820
but its standard equipment for space shuttle crews.

333
00:28:37,680 --> 00:28:42,180
In the 1950s, it even played a role in the Cold War.

334
00:28:42,980 --> 00:28:50,000
One of my favorite stories about the Swiss Army knife is when Francis Gary Powers and the U-2 plane was shot down over Russia.

335
00:28:50,880 --> 00:28:55,480
And Russia, of course, then was our mortal enemy, and they trotted him out in front of the cameras.

336
00:28:56,060 --> 00:29:01,500
And one of the things they showed was the Swiss Army knife, and they labeled it CIA equipment.

337
00:29:02,400 --> 00:29:07,000
As practical as Carl Elzener's gadget has been over the years,

338
00:29:07,660 --> 00:29:11,840
another gadgeteer would become famous for being anything but practical.

339
00:29:14,280 --> 00:29:19,620
Hired in 1857, Civil War nurse and founder of the Red Cross,

340
00:29:19,920 --> 00:29:25,440
Clara Barton became one of the first women ever employed by the U.S. Patent Office.

341
00:29:26,520 --> 00:29:29,240
Gadgets will return on Modern Marvels.

342
00:29:31,500 --> 00:29:36,960
Moments away from the crowning of Hamaker Schlemmer's search for invention grand prize winner,

343
00:29:37,620 --> 00:29:42,240
judges circulate amongst the gadgeteers, finalizing their decisions.

344
00:29:43,260 --> 00:29:46,740
They're also getting a glimpse at the future of gadgetry.

345
00:29:46,960 --> 00:29:49,540
More of a high-tech lifestyle is what we're moving towards.

346
00:29:49,820 --> 00:29:52,160
People look to these gadgets to make their life simpler,

347
00:29:52,161 --> 00:29:58,400
but the minute we make our lives simpler, then we stretch ourselves further to fill in those gaps we've created.

348
00:29:58,401 --> 00:30:06,100
A case in point are the gadgets invented by this San Francisco physician and this team of inventors from San Diego.

349
00:30:07,640 --> 00:30:15,460
Lou Arnell, his dad Leon, and best friend Tom Sturgis pulled their talents to invent the executive door closer.

350
00:30:15,940 --> 00:30:21,140
Its function is to create privacy at the touch of a button.

351
00:30:21,141 --> 00:30:30,060
The executive door closer brings the convenience of remote control to the cumbersome task of opening and closing doors.

352
00:30:30,460 --> 00:30:34,680
I'm an attorney, therefore I have one of these in my office and I use it all the time.

353
00:30:34,920 --> 00:30:37,140
I often need for confidentiality and privacy.

354
00:30:37,940 --> 00:30:41,700
The time and energy savings can then be dedicated to exercise.

355
00:30:42,480 --> 00:30:45,900
And Dr. Robert Sineko has just the gadget for that.

356
00:30:45,901 --> 00:30:48,520
The audio feedback

357
00:30:48,521 --> 00:30:50,600
AvTrainer offers a musical

358
00:30:50,601 --> 00:30:52,960
reminder to suck in your gut

359
00:30:52,961 --> 00:30:54,560
You take your personal listening device

360
00:30:54,561 --> 00:30:56,480
which might be a Walkman

361
00:30:56,481 --> 00:30:58,640
or a CD player, you hang it on the bell

362
00:30:58,641 --> 00:31:00,640
you plug this ordinary

363
00:31:00,641 --> 00:31:02,280
pair of headphones in through

364
00:31:02,281 --> 00:31:04,820
the sensor switch

365
00:31:04,821 --> 00:31:06,300
on the bell so that

366
00:31:06,301 --> 00:31:07,960
if you're walking around like this

367
00:31:07,961 --> 00:31:10,300
with your gut in the way you should

368
00:31:10,301 --> 00:31:11,700
tucking in your tummy

369
00:31:11,701 --> 00:31:14,080
the sound through the headphones is wonderful

370
00:31:14,081 --> 00:31:25,296
On the other hand, if you forget, and we all forget sometimes, and let your stomach pouch out so that you're not strengthening and toning those muscles, you do this,

371
00:31:25,297 --> 00:31:26,520
where's the sound?

372
00:31:26,820 --> 00:31:31,180
You can still hear it, but it doesn't sound good. It sounds thin. It sounds tinny.

373
00:31:31,860 --> 00:31:41,080
Such gadgets are prime examples of how, at the dawn of the new millennium, we have come to call on technology to assist us in doing just about anything.

374
00:31:41,081 --> 00:31:41,786
anything.

375
00:31:41,787 --> 00:31:48,380
But a hundred years ago, a true hero amongst gadgeteers was already envisioning such

376
00:31:48,381 --> 00:31:52,508
a world. His name was Rube Goldberg.

377
00:31:52,509 --> 00:31:58,700
He was the only living person to ever have his name in

378
00:31:58,701 --> 00:32:01,269
a dictionary as an adjective.

379
00:32:01,270 --> 00:32:06,760
The definition is when you overcomplicate a simple act. A Rube

380
00:32:06,761 --> 00:32:13,760
Goldberg is anything that is too complicated to be true. Except it works.

381
00:32:13,761 --> 00:32:22,760
Reuben Lucius Goldberg was born in Northern California in 1883 to a father who insisted he become an engineer.

382
00:32:22,761 --> 00:32:31,760
After college, Goldberg complied, but six months later he quit to pursue his dream of becoming an artist.

383
00:32:31,761 --> 00:32:43,360
He supported himself as a sports cartoonist for a San Francisco newspaper before moving to New York at age 23 in 1906.

384
00:32:45,060 --> 00:32:51,500
At that time, Americans were imagining the unlimited possibilities of machines.

385
00:32:51,501 --> 00:33:00,180
I think the whole idea of the machine age and everything else sort of caught up with him,

386
00:33:00,260 --> 00:33:05,060
and he sort of felt that people were getting too dependent on gadgets.

387
00:33:05,920 --> 00:33:13,600
And whereas he himself loved gadgets, he had cars, he loved photography, things like that.

388
00:33:14,540 --> 00:33:20,800
In New York, Goldberg became a prolific cartoonist, drawing dozens of different comic strips.

389
00:33:21,500 --> 00:33:24,700
He soon became the highest paid cartoonist in the nation.

390
00:33:25,280 --> 00:33:28,860
His most popular cartoons involved his alter ego,

391
00:33:29,660 --> 00:33:33,580
Professor Butz, an inventor of outrageous gadgets.

392
00:33:34,040 --> 00:33:38,180
He thought that everybody was really sort of getting overcomplicated

393
00:33:38,181 --> 00:33:40,180
in terms of their lives,

394
00:33:40,560 --> 00:33:43,420
in terms of the things that they were buying and using.

395
00:33:43,980 --> 00:33:49,520
So his inventions were doing things the hard way.

396
00:33:49,521 --> 00:33:52,060
He was over-complicating a simple act.

397
00:33:52,780 --> 00:33:58,620
And it wasn't meant as a humorist, and it was meant as satire,

398
00:33:58,980 --> 00:34:03,340
but it wasn't meant as an aggressive put-down of the machine age.

399
00:34:04,040 --> 00:34:09,480
Goldberg's Professor Butts cartoons were so popular in the first half of the century

400
00:34:09,481 --> 00:34:15,960
that in 1995, the U.S. Postal Service honored him with a postage stamp.

401
00:34:15,961 --> 00:34:21,980
The image is one of his best-known gadgets, the self-operating napkin.

402
00:34:21,981 --> 00:34:34,440
The irony of Goldberg is that while he was an artist, a sculptor, wrote magazine articles, and even several popular songs, he never actually made one of his gadgets.

403
00:34:34,441 --> 00:34:40,440
However, he used his engineering background to assure that if somebody followed his plans,

404
00:34:40,820 --> 00:34:47,020
and in some cases, if the porcupine performed accordingly, the gadget would work.

405
00:34:48,400 --> 00:34:53,200
Once a year, Goldberg's imaginative engineering and dedication to gadgetry

406
00:34:53,201 --> 00:34:57,100
is celebrated at Purdue University in Indiana.

407
00:34:57,760 --> 00:35:04,320
It is there that the Theta Tau Engineering Fraternity sponsors a Rube Goldberg contest

408
00:35:04,920 --> 00:35:08,920
The goal is to create a device to perform the simplest of acts,

409
00:35:09,020 --> 00:35:11,620
such as applying toothpaste to a toothbrush,

410
00:35:12,040 --> 00:35:14,440
in no fewer than 20 steps.

411
00:35:15,620 --> 00:35:20,600
While few, if any, of these Goldberg devices will find their way to the marketplace,

412
00:35:21,420 --> 00:35:23,860
they're marvels that celebrate gadgetry.

413
00:35:24,460 --> 00:35:26,800
And that's all Goldberg ever wanted to do.

414
00:35:27,840 --> 00:35:30,780
Rube didn't invent gadgets, but he celebrated gadgets.

415
00:35:30,781 --> 00:35:39,540
He celebrated them in a wonderful way because he pointed out to you that gadgets were great

416
00:35:39,541 --> 00:35:45,300
as long as you didn't let them overcomplicate your life too much or dominate your life too much.

417
00:35:46,380 --> 00:35:54,680
And I think for that point of view, definitely he was a missionary of gadgets and a celebrant.

418
00:35:55,720 --> 00:35:59,580
Goldberg's inventiveness has made his name immortal.

419
00:36:00,480 --> 00:36:03,480
Today it describes not only elaborate gadgets,

420
00:36:04,080 --> 00:36:09,300
but everything from the U.S. tax code to bureaucratic plans for building highways.

421
00:36:10,260 --> 00:36:13,700
Goldberg died in 1970 at age 77.

422
00:36:14,560 --> 00:36:16,160
And when he did...

423
00:36:16,161 --> 00:36:18,600
If you know someone that can't chew too well...

424
00:36:18,601 --> 00:36:24,320
...the Gadget King of the second half of the century had already inherited the throne.

425
00:36:24,321 --> 00:36:34,400
In 1939, Rube Goldberg reinvented himself as a serious political cartoonist for the New York Sun

426
00:36:34,401 --> 00:36:38,460
and was awarded the Pulitzer Prize in 1947.

427
00:36:39,640 --> 00:36:41,440
Modern Marvels will return.

428
00:36:43,020 --> 00:36:49,240
With the grand prize winner of the Hamaker-Schlemmer Search for Invention contest about to be announced,

429
00:36:49,740 --> 00:36:52,520
the contestants nervously await the decision.

430
00:36:52,521 --> 00:36:59,520
Will they give a nod to one of the many high-tech gadgets in the contest, or reward a more utilitarian

431
00:37:00,520 --> 00:37:03,175
gadget? Edwin Rodriguez hopes so.

432
00:37:03,176 --> 00:37:07,520
Rodriguez is a chef in a New York restaurant where the

433
00:37:07,521 --> 00:37:11,520
often difficult to peel plantain is a staple of the menu.

434
00:37:11,521 --> 00:37:14,351
We do all kinds of things with them.

435
00:37:14,352 --> 00:37:18,520
So you can fry them, bake them, boil them, mash them.

436
00:37:18,521 --> 00:37:21,240
so it's very nice.

437
00:37:21,860 --> 00:37:25,840
Rodriguez made his job much easier when he invented and patented

438
00:37:25,841 --> 00:37:30,120
a completely unique and effective plantain peeler.

439
00:37:31,120 --> 00:37:33,700
As a butcher in a New Jersey grocery store,

440
00:37:34,060 --> 00:37:38,540
James Mick was also in a unique position to spot and solve a problem.

441
00:37:39,640 --> 00:37:42,740
His shopping cart canopy keeps groceries dry

442
00:37:42,741 --> 00:37:45,480
as they're pushed to the car on rainy days.

443
00:37:46,560 --> 00:37:48,300
A one, two, three operation.

444
00:37:48,520 --> 00:37:58,280
Whoever wins, there is a good chance that they might have to spend the prize money on the daunting task of bringing their invention to the marketplace.

